WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.755
Cloud visibility depends on logs and telemetry. In a cloud environment, many important actions happen through APIs and automation.

2
00:00:07.875 --> 00:00:14.406
Resources can be created, changed, exposed, tagged, moved, or deleted without anyone logging into a server.

3
00:00:14.526 --> 00:00:23.097
If logging is missing or too short-lived, teams may not be able to answer basic questions during review or response.

4
00:00:23.217 --> 00:00:34.238
Useful cloud logs come from several layers. Control-plane logs record management actions such as creating resources, changing policies, modifying network rules, or disabling a service setting.

5
00:00:34.358 --> 00:00:42.521
Identity logs show sign-ins, token use, role assumptions, failed authentication, and changes to users, groups, roles, or service accounts.

6
00:00:42.641 --> 00:00:50.804
Network logs show connection patterns and rejected traffic. Storage access logs can show reads, writes, deletes, and unusual access volume.

7
00:00:50.924 --> 00:00:57.047
Application logs and workload telemetry explain what the system did after traffic reached the application.

8
00:00:57.167 --> 00:01:02.881
Good logs help answer who did what, from where, to which resource, and when.

9
00:01:03.001 --> 00:01:08.307
Those questions are valuable for troubleshooting, compliance, incident response, and ordinary engineering review.

10
00:01:08.427 --> 00:01:15.774
They also support accountability. A team cannot improve a baseline if it cannot see when the baseline changes.

11
00:01:15.894 --> 00:01:31.404
Monitoring should focus on meaningful risk. Useful signals include unusual identity activity, new public exposure, broad policy changes, disabled logging, unexpected data access spikes, repeated failed login patterns, suspicious resource creation, and configuration drift from a known baseline.

12
00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:37.647
Not every event needs an alert. Too much noise teaches teams to ignore the system.

13
00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:47.562
The best detection rules are tied to decisions: investigate, notify an owner, open a ticket, roll back a change, or start an incident process.

14
00:01:47.682 --> 00:01:57.478
Logs must be enabled, retained, and protected. A log that can be changed by the same identity being monitored is weaker than it looks.

15
00:01:57.598 --> 00:02:03.313
Security-relevant logs should have controlled access, appropriate retention, and protection from casual deletion.

16
00:02:03.433 --> 00:02:13.637
Retention should balance investigation needs, privacy, cost, and policy requirements. Teams should test whether they can retrieve and understand the logs before an urgent event.

17
00:02:13.757 --> 00:02:17.838
Detection is not only a tool. It is a workflow.

18
00:02:17.958 --> 00:02:25.713
Someone must own alerts, tune them, review them, document findings, and improve controls based on what the logs reveal.

19
00:02:25.833 --> 00:02:35.221
Cloud security becomes stronger when visibility is treated as part of the system design from the beginning, not added after something goes wrong.
